Read CLAUDE.md.

Implement Sprint 2.1: the sequence engine (email, task, call steps).
Do NOT send real email yet — Postal is Sprint 2.2.

Deliverables:

1. Sequence model: id, tenant_id, name, steps jsonb (array of
   {type, config, delay_hours}), status. Step types: email, task, call
   (manual log only). Each email step has template_id.
   sequence_template table: id, tenant_id, subject, body_html, body_text,
   variables jsonb.

2. sequence_enrollment: id, tenant_id, sequence_id, contact_id,
   opportunity_id NULL, status ('active','paused','completed','replied',
   'bounced'), current_step int, next_run_at timestamptz, created_at.

3. Execution via Temporal: one Temporal workflow per enrollment.
   Workflow loop: wait until next_run_at → execute step → compute next
   next_run_at → repeat until status != active. Every step execution is
   a Temporal Activity so it is durable across worker restarts.
   Activities call internal/sequence/executor.go which writes to
   outbox (topic `ratify.sequence.step`, dispatched to a sender in
   Sprint 2.2) OR logs the task/call for the rep.

4. Send throttling: per-tenant rate limit (configurable, default 50/min),
   per-domain rate limit (default 20/hour), send-window enforcement (per
   tenant config: hour range + timezone). All limits in Redis with
   sliding windows. A step that hits a limit reschedules rather than
   fails.

5. Reply and bounce detection: reply on the contact's email (received via
   a webhook endpoint to be wired in Sprint 2.2) sets enrollment status
   'replied' and stops the workflow. Same for bounce.

6. Pause/resume: user can pause an enrollment at any time; the workflow
   blocks on a Temporal signal until resumed or the sequence is stopped.

7. AI draft variants: POST /api/v1/sequences/{id}/steps/{n}/draft returns
   an LLM-drafted subject + body based on the contact and account context.
   This goes through the decision gate as a READ action (no CRM write,
   no send). Stored on the step config only if the user saves it.

8. Tests:
   - A 3-step sequence with 1-hour delays runs in ~2 hours in test mode
     (configurable delay divisor for tests).
   - Worker restart mid-workflow resumes exactly where it left off, no
     duplicate step execution.
   - Throttle exceeded → step rescheduled, not failed.
   - Reply detection stops the workflow within 5 seconds.

Acceptance criteria:
- `make test` green.
- A single enrollment can be started, paused, resumed, and completed.
- No email is actually sent in Sprint 2.1 — the outbox has
  `ratify.sequence.step` events but no consumer sends them.
- Temporal workflow history is queryable by enrollment ID.

Commit when green.